[
  {
    "marker": "C1",
    "citation": "crexx-rag:library-61405eb8183ffd16da950f0d:source-sha256:592ffedcb177c22e1c9e8c4603a19b818e3df9398d4dc1c49503f8545f2d4664:revision-sha256:61ab16fde300893bdf7d4cbc332e42c2c1ab0eddf239098215399859a24ec21e:utf8-502-951",
    "text": "The Bore Stone, in which Bruce planted his standard before the battle in which he defeated Edward II. in 1314 (see below), is preserved by an iron grating. A mile to the west is the Gillies’ Hill, now finely wooded, over which the Scots’ camp-followers appeared to complete the discomfiture of the English, to which event it owes its name. Bannockburn House was Prince Charles Edward’s headquarters in January 1746 before the fight at Falkirk.",
